- it can be tested or reviewed independently;
- its file surface is mostly non-overlapping with sibling lanes;
- shared contracts are already approved or represented by an upstream gate;
- integration back into the product is explicit.

Reject fake parallelism. Do not split lanes that secretly edit the same files, same hunks, same API contract, same schema, or an unresolved architecture boundary. If candidate lanes overlap, serialize them or create/complete a prerequisite contract or integration gate first, then split.

Worktree assignment rule:
- Once a lane passes independence validation, create one branch/worktree for that lane.
- Name branches clearly after the lane, preferably `lane/<short-lane-name>`.
- Keep all edits for that lane inside its worktree.
- Do not edit sibling-lane files from this lane's worktree.
- Do not let two worktrees edit the same file unless a prior integration or contract gate explicitly allows it.

Suggested worktree pattern from the main repo root:

```powershell
git worktree add -b lane/<short-lane-name> ../worktrees/<short-lane-name> HEAD
```

Execution model:
- One lane = one worktree = one isolated workstream.
- Tickets inside a lane remain sequential according to the lane dependency chain.
- Separate lanes may run in parallel only when their owned files and contracts do not overlap.
- Heavy builds/tests should not run simultaneously on a laptop. Use narrow checks per lane and milestone/full verification at integration gates.

Required preflight report before implementation:

```text
LANE MAP
- Lane name:
- Product/module goal:
- Worktree path:
- Branch name:
- Lane-root card:
- Child cards:
- Owned files/directories:
- Forbidden/overlap files/directories:
- Project scope:
- Upstream prerequisites:
- Downstream integration gate:
- Can run in parallel with:
- Must not overlap with:
- Verification command:
- Independence verdict: PASS/FAIL
- If FAIL: serialize or name the missing contract/integration gate.
```

Implementation rules:
- Finish only the current lane card(s).
- Do not bundle future-lane work.
- Do not create fake runtime product data.
- Test doubles/fixtures are allowed only for tests and contract validation.
- Do not push.
- Do not mark Done; Hermes alone owns Done.
- When a lane card is ready, post or prepare a report beginning exactly `READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:`.

Hermes lane review report:

```text
READY FOR HERMES REVIEW:
- card ID/title:
- lane name:
- worktree path:
- branch name:
- files changed:
- exact tests/checks run and output:
- commit SHA if committed:
- blockers/risks:
- downstream cards believed unblocked:
```

Integration gates:
- After sibling lanes complete, do not assume the product works.
- Rejoin lanes only through the explicit downstream integration gate.
- The integration gate must verify actual product/API behavior, not only isolated lane success.

## User-Provided Dependency Streams

If the user provides dependency streams, parse and use them as the initial stream map, then verify against Kanban before dispatch.

Accepted shapes include:

```text
STREAM A: Backend/API
- t_aaa: Add schema
- t_bbb: Add route
- t_ccc: Add route tests

STREAM B: Frontend/UI
- t_ddd: Add types
- t_eee: Add component
- t_fff: Add UI tests

GATES:
- t_ggg: integration test waits for STREAM A + STREAM B
```

```text
Parallel lanes:
1. t_123 -> t_456 -> t_789
2. t_abc -> t_def
3. t_xyz
Gate: t_gate waits for lane terminals.
```

When streams are provided:
1. Normalize them into the stream map.
   - If the stream tasks do not exist on Kanban, or the cards were planned but not created/promoted, report the mismatch and stop. Do not execute against uncreated cards.
2. Check each task exists on Kanban and is explicitly marked `[Codex]` before treating it as implementable by Codex.
3. Check parent/child links, statuses, and declared project scope.
4. Detect mismatches between user streams and Kanban.
5. If mismatch is minor, report/comment it and follow Kanban.
6. If mismatch changes execution safety, ticket ownership, or project-scope authorization, post a process-blocker comment, keep the same card In Progress, and continue safe in-scope investigation while routing the exact decision.
7. Dispatch one subagent/workstream per safe `[Codex]` stream root at goal start.

Copy-paste template for users:

```text
DEPENDENCY STREAMS FOR CODEX

Board:
<board>

Workspace:
<path>

Review policy:
<who owns Done / handoff format>

Parallel streams:

Stream 1 - <name>
- Current/root: <task_id> - <title>
- Then: <task_id> - <title>
- Lane owns:
- Lane must not touch:
- Verification:

Stream 2 - <name>
- Current/root:
- Then:
- Lane owns:
- Lane must not touch:
- Verification:

Integration gates:
- <task_id> waits for <stream terminals>

Instructions:
- Verify these streams against Kanban.
- Dispatch one subagent/workstream per safe current/root stream immediately.
- Do not start gates until stream terminals are approved.
```

## Mandatory Parallelism Policy

At any safe boundary, if two or more independent graph-unblocked tasks/streams exist, launch parallel subagents/workstreams.

Safe boundaries:
- goal start;
- after board/stream ingestion;
- after a ticket handoff;
- after a review approval or block;
- after resolving a blocker;
- before claiming next work;
- on every 1-minute Kanban poll;
- when restarting after stall;
- when the user, reviewer, or Kanban sends a heartbeat.

Decision rule:

```text
if blocked_codex_tickets_in_project_scope >= 1:
    transition the highest-priority/current blocked card only to In Progress
    resume its original worker or dispatch one continuity replacement
elif current_codex_card_is_triage_due_to_block_loop_detected:
    recover only that same card to In Progress
    resume its original worker or dispatch one continuity replacement
elif ready_or_running_codex_tickets_in_project_scope == 0:
    report no Kanban-backed `[Codex]` work inside project scope and stop
elif safe_parallel_streams >= 2:
    dispatch one PowerShell lane orchestrator per safe stream
else:
    work the single stream or route the blocker
```

Acceptable reasons not to parallelize:
- file/hunk overlap;
- shared public contract unresolved;
- schema/migration ordering;
- architecture boundary unresolved;
- dirty worktree prevents safe isolation;
- resource constraints for heavy tests/builds;
- reviewer/user explicitly directed serial execution;
- only one graph-unblocked stream exists.

Unacceptable reasons:
- "simpler";
- "not necessary";
- "I can do it myself";
- "I forgot";
- "coordination overhead";
- "parallel agents might be overkill" without a concrete safety reason.

If parallelism is available but not dispatched, record:

```text
PARALLELISM EXCEPTION:
- streams considered:
- graph-unblocked tasks:
- reason parallelism is unsafe:
- conflicts:
- selected single stream:
- what would make parallelism safe:
```

Post this as a task comment or heartbeat when the workflow has a Kanban flight recorder and it is appropriate.

